ved in the last cabinet the prime  minister is I think the longest serving  cabinet minister  of all of them and the chancellor is  also a long-serving cabinet minister too  I'm afraid it's wearing thin to say that  the seventh plan is going to be better  than the other six there is a pattern of  stagnation there's no point in blaming  Europe for it we've had a pattern of  stagnation on economic growth in this  country which has been presided over by  the conservatives and that's why they  are not the change that the country  needs you talked about the silencing of  the obr  um the obr often when there's a where  then there's a budget will produce an  analysis won't they of what it means for  the economy and how much it's going to  cost when I spoke to Simon Clark he said  it wasn't about silencing them he said  there wasn't enough time to pull  together a report or what do you make of  that honestly I think that's nonsense  um the what are they frightened of the  lbr are under a statutory duty to  produce a report uh twice a year we're  not today's event isn't termed our  budget but to be honest the measures in  it are as big as any budget may be  bigger so it is perfectly right that the  obr should have produced a report one  has to  assume that the government are  frightened of something that might have  been in that but there's a more  important point about this this point  about institutional stability  it's not just about the lbr it's about  flip-flops on policy this is the third  change in National Insurance in six  months that's been announced yesterday  we've got a tug of war with the bank of  England where they've had to issue a  warning to the government yesterday that  having raised interest rates by half a  percent they will return and do more in  November depending on what's announced  today and then you've got the firing of  the permanent secretary at the treasury  if you take all these things together  they do not send out a message of  confidence an